g01/concept/g01-anatomy/card:
Every commit has:

- a **hash**: a 40-character ID computed from its content (`git log` shows it in full; `d0469eb`, the first 7 characters, is enough to name it);
- a **snapshot** of every file;
- its **parent**: the commit before it (the root commit has none);
- the **author** and **date**, and the **message**.

Because each commit points to its parent, the commits form a chain: the **history**. Watch the 🌳 Graph panel: each dot is a commit, and the line goes to its parent.

`git log` lists commits newest first; `git log --oneline` shows one line each; `git show` shows the latest commit and exactly what it changed.

g02/concept/g02-names/card:
To look at (or undo) a commit, you name it. Three ways:

- its **hash**: the full 40 characters, or just the first 7 (`git log --oneline` prints those);
- **`HEAD`**: the commit you're on now (the newest one on your branch);
- **`HEAD~1`**: HEAD's parent, one step back; `HEAD~2` is two steps back, and so on.

`git show` displays one commit: its details, then exactly which lines it added (`+`) and removed (`-`). Without a name it shows HEAD. `git show --stat` lists just the files.

g05/concept/g05-tracking/card:
Your repository keeps a bookmark of each hub branch: **`origin/main`**, a *remote-tracking branch*. It shows where the hub's `main` was **the last time you talked to the hub** (clone, fetch, pull or push).

`git status` compares your `main` with that bookmark:

- **ahead by N**: you have N commits the hub doesn't (push them);
- **behind by N**: the hub has N you don't (pull them);
- **diverged**: both.

The bookmark doesn't update by itself. If Sam pushed a minute ago, your status still says "up to date" until you fetch.

g06/concept/g06-feature/card:
Teams keep `main` working at all times. Every change, however small, is made on its own **feature branch**:

1. `git switch -c footer` (from an up-to-date `main`: pull first);
2. commit your work there, as many commits as you need;
3. `git push -u origin footer`: put the branch on the hub.

Pushing a branch doesn't change `main`, so you can share unfinished work safely. Name branches after what they do: `footer`, `fix-typo`, `contact-page`.

g06/concept/g06-messages/card:
Your history is read by teammates (and by you, in six months). A good commit message:

- **subject line** (the first line) says what the commit does, in the imperative: *"Add footer"*, *"Fix typo in the price list"*, not *"stuff"*, *"fix"* or *"changes"*;
- keeps the subject short (about 50 characters), so `git log --oneline` stays readable;
- optionally a blank line, then a **body** saying *why*.

One change per commit (module 1) makes good messages easy to write. Pull request titles follow the same rules.
